  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Iran's negotiating team formally suspended dialogues and exchange of texts through mediators on June 1, with state media outlet Tasnim attributing the halt to ongoing Israeli military operations in Lebanon, which Iran characterized as violations of ceasefire preconditions. The suspension received independent confirmation from the Washington Post, NPR, Washington Times, and Euronews, each citing Iranian officials. Trump publicly contradicted the suspension, asserting talks continued at a "rapid pace," but no Iranian-side confirmation of resumed contact appeared in any monitored source by the morning of June 2; the Iranian MFA offered no acceptance of Trump's May 31 tightened language on uranium retrieval mechanics and Hormuz wording. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>The weekend of May 31–June 1 produced simultaneous bilateral military exchanges over the Hormuz zone. US Central Command struck Iranian radar and command-and-control sites at Goruk on the mainland and on Qeshm Island inside the Strait; the IRGC's aerospace force issued a same-day public attribution statement claiming it had targeted the originating US air base, and separately fired missiles at American forces in Kuwait. AIS trackers continued to record approximately 4 active commercial vessels in the Strait — below 5% of pre-war baseline — with no movement toward the 50-vessel thresholds. <span class="cite">F1</span> (Hormuz by June 10) is retired as OBE effective today: mine-clearing timelines physically foreclose the June 10 threshold regardless of diplomatic outcome. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F6</span></p>

      <p>The Knesset dissolution bill cleared its first plenum reading on June 1 with a unanimous 106-0 vote, following an 8-0 House Committee endorsement earlier the same day. The result resolves the uncertainty flagged at Day 94 and confirms full coalition alignment at the first-reading stage. The bill returns to committee for preparation of the second and third readings; no election date has been inserted into bill text, and the internal coalition dispute between haredi factions preferring September and Netanyahu's preference for late October remains open — a structural obstacle under Israeli procedure, since an election date must be embedded before the second reading proceeds. Separately, polling from late May projects Netanyahu's bloc at 49-52 seats, well below the 61-seat threshold, while the sitting coalition holds approximately 60 seats under contested methodology. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span>; supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>

      <p>Lebanon's military track continued to escalate on June 1 in ways that complicated the June 2-3 Washington talks confirmed as scheduled. Netanyahu ordered strikes on Beirut's southern suburbs and Israeli ground forces crossed the Litani River — the deepest incursion since 2000 — with the IDF characterizing the advance as a tactical success. Trump announced a partial ceasefire arrangement in which Israeli strikes on Beirut suburbs would halt in exchange for Hezbollah restraint, but Netanyahu stated publicly that the military "will continue to operate as planned," and Lebanon's state news agency confirmed overnight strikes continued. Washington's proposal on the table describes a permanent de-escalation framework — a more demanding deliverable than a simple 60-day rollover — and June 2-3 talks proceeded without a communique observable by June 2 morning. → moved <span class="cite">F7</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:58%;">Escalation 58%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:30%;">Protracted 30%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:12%;">De-esc 12%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> Iran's negotiation suspension (F3 at floor, F4 at 18%) combined with active bilateral military exchanges over Hormuz (F6 at 20%) and IDF Litani crossing (F7 at 50%) converge toward a scenario in which no diplomatic framework closes before June 7, Lebanon talks produce no communique, and kinetic activity over the Strait persists through June.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> Neither a diplomatic breakthrough nor further dramatic kinetic escalation materializes before July 31; Iran's suspension/resumption pattern reasserts itself in late June, talks resume but do not close, Hormuz vessel counts remain under 10/day, and the Lebanon ceasefire operates in a de facto but not formally extended state.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> Iranian MFA confirms resumed text exchange by June 4-5, June 2-3 Washington talks produce a formal Lebanon extension communique, and F4 closes near-YES — a path requiring simultaneous reversal of multiple confirmed adverse indicators within 72 hours.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media framing on June 1-2 centered on two coordinated narratives: the formal justification for suspending US negotiations and the military narrative of successful retaliation. Tasnim and state-aligned outlets cited the Israeli ground advance into Lebanon and strikes on Beirut as proof that the existing ceasefire framework had been "violated on all fronts," providing the formal rationale for the negotiation suspension. This framing aligns with Iran's established diplomatic posture — that Lebanese front stability constitutes a precondition for any Hormuz or nuclear framework discussions — a linkage Khamenei's office has maintained publicly across multiple prior rounds. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>The IRGC public relations apparatus issued a same-day attribution statement claiming its aerospace force targeted the US air base from which assets that struck sites in Hormozgan province had originated — a marked departure from Iran's typical practice of delayed or ambiguous attribution. The speed and specificity of the claim appear calibrated to domestic messaging around deterrence credibility rather than to outward diplomatic communication. Prior suspension episodes (January 2025, September 2025) were not accompanied by simultaneous strikes on Iranian territory, making the current combination qualitatively distinct even as the diplomatic suspension language tracks prior precedent. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>On the nuclear negotiation track, Iranian state media continued to frame the core sticking points — uranium enrichment rights, disposition of highly enriched uranium stockpiles, and conditions sequencing for sanctions relief — as matters of national sovereignty not subject to external revision. What is confirmed as of June 2 is the formal suspension of text exchange through mediators and the absence of any Iranian MFA acceptance of Trump's May 31 tightened language; what remains unresolved is whether the suspension constitutes a tactical pause consistent with prior cycles or a more durable break. Five days remain to the June 7 <span class="cite">F4</span> horizon. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Khamenei's publicly reconstructed posture remains unchanged: uranium enrichment as non-negotiable, the Hormuz closure as a pressure instrument against adversaries. Iranian framing of the broader regional situation continues to treat Hormuz, Lebanon, and Gaza as an integrated pressure system — a construct that allows escalation on any single front to justify suspension across all diplomatic tracks simultaneously, and that complicates any US effort to compartmentalize nuclear talks from the Lebanon military trajectory. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

